      "ImageDescription": {
        "value": "<b>Reich Youth Day in Potsdam, 1932.</b> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adolf_Hitler\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Adolf Hitler\">Adolf Hitler</a> at the first Reich Youth Day (German: <i>Erster nationalsozialistischer Reichsjugendtag</i>, 1st annual Hitler Youth rally) in <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Potsdam\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Potsdam\">Potsdam</a> on October 2, 1932, alongside <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baldur_von_Schirach\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Baldur von Schirach\">Baldur von Schirach</a> (at the far left), leader of the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nazi_Party\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Nazi Party\">Nazi Party</a>'s <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hitler_Youth\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Hitler Youth\">Hitler Youth</a> organization (German: <a href=\"https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hitlerjugend\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"de:Hitlerjugend\">Hitlerjugend</a>, HJ). HJ boys march in a parade before a large crowd of spectators and fellow HJ members at the Stadium during the National Socialist event. Sennecke press photograph.<br>\nAfter a brief power struggle, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gerhard_Ro%C3%9Fbach\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Gerhard Ro\u00dfbach\">Gerhard Ro\u00dfbach</a>'s <i><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schilljugend\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Schilljugend\">Schilljugend</a> was overtaken by <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kurt_Gruber\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Kurt Gruber\">Kurt Gruber</a>, leading to the establishment of the Nazi Party's official youth organization, which was renamed Hitler-Jugend (</i>Hitler Youth<i>) in 1926. By 1930, it had over 25,000 members, with a junior branch, </i><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deutsches_Jungvolk\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Deutsches Jungvolk\">Deutsches Jungvolk</a>, for boys aged 10-14 and the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/League_of_German_Girls\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:League of German Girls\">League of German Girls</a> (BDM) for girls aged 10-18. In 1932, Chancellor<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heinrich_Br%C3%BCning\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Heinrich Br\u00fcning\">Heinrich Br\u00fcning</a> banned the movement, but his successor <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franz_von_Papen\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Franz von Papen\">Franz von Papen</a> lifted the ban. The first Hitler Youth rally, held in Potsdam on October 1 and 2, 1932, saw 80,000 boys and 20,000 girls attend. The event led to the Hitler Youth's formal inclusion in the Reich Committee of German Youth Associations in the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weimar_Republic\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Weimar Republic\">Weimar Republic</a>, in which all German youth organizations had voluntarily united, and the Potsdam Badge (<i><a href=\"https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Potsdam-Abzeichen_der_NSDAP\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"de:Potsdam-Abzeichen der NSDAP\">Potsdam-Abzeichen der NSDAP</a></i>) was introduced to commemorate the rally.",
        "source": "commons-desc-page"
      },
